\section{Separate the Current Frame from the Current Top}

A stack interpreter needs to find the current code, instruction
pointer, and locals even as evaluating expressions pushes and
pops temporary values. Use a frame descriptor with a code or
closure reference, the next instruction, the base of its local
slots, and the segment boundary to restore on return. In this
design a separate frame vector indexes descriptors and a value
vector holds locals and operands; some VMs instead put metadata
on the same stack. O'Leary and Feeley's Ribbit is a compact
stack-machine contrast, using linked three-field cells for
stack, bytecode, and objects rather than the vectors used here.
The layout is an implementation choice, not a change to
call-by-value execution.

Assume a call has already computed its callee and arguments.
The caller's continuation, including its next instruction,
is saved before transferring control. Reserve enough space for
callee locals and operands, consistently marking uninitialized
slots so the collector does not read uninitialized memory as a
value. On normal return restore the caller's previous operand
depth, consuming the call inputs and pushing one result. Locals
are addressed relative to the frame base; an absolute stack
index identifies a particular live activation, even when two
recursive calls have the same source declaration and local
number.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
Record & Typical lookup & Lifetime \\
\hline
Frame descriptor & Current activation & Until return or unwind \\
Local slot & Frame base plus slot number & At most the region owning the slot \\
Operand slot & Top relative to frame boundary & Until consumed or discarded \\
Upvalue cell & Closure's capture index & While the cell is reachable \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\section{Capture a Mutable Variable Once Per Activation}

Consider a factory returning two closures, \texttt{read} and
\texttt{bump}, over one local initialized to 40. If each
closure captures the value by copying 40 into a private field,
calling \texttt{bump} does not affect \texttt{read}. For
capture by reference, both closures must keep the same
indirect cell. The cell can refer to the local slot while it
is live and contain its own value after the slot dies. As
shown in \Cref{fig:visual-427}, the transition changes the
cell's storage target but not the identities in the closure
records. Call the closed representation a heap slot, not a
pointer into the dead frame.

