% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Close Cells Before Reusing Slots}

\begin{verbatim}
capture(activation, local):
    i := activation.base + local
    require i names a live local in this activation
    if open_index.contains(i): return open_index[i]
    cell := new_upvalue(Open(stack_id, i))
    open_index[i] := cell
    return cell

close_suffix(first_invalid):
    # open_index is ordered by slot, for a single stack.
    while not open_index.empty():
        i, cell := open_index.max_pair()
        if i < first_invalid: break
        value := stack[i]           # still live here
        publish_closed(cell, value) # includes heap barrier if needed
        open_index.remove(i)

return_from_call():
    frame := frames.last()
    result := root_temporarily(pop_result())
    close_suffix(frame.restore_depth)
    stack.truncate(frame.restore_depth)
    frames.pop()
    push(result)
\end{verbatim}

Closing the cell copies the current value, not the initial
value at capture. During a call, assignments to a captured
local can go through its slot because an open upvalue's reads
and writes use that same slot. After close, both closure
operations use the closed value field. To capture a variable
already captured by a parent closure, forward the parent's
cell rather than interning a new stack index. Use a stack ID
or a per-stack index if different coroutines can have live
slots at the same numeric offset. A raw pointer into a vector
is not safe for open cells if the vector may relocate on push.

Slot reuse is the actual boundary. A block-local slot reused
before frame return must close at block exit, before writing
any other variable there. A block that invalidates a non-suffix
set of slots needs \texttt{close\_slot} calls or an allocator
policy that releases blocks in stack order. Normal return is
not the only release path: exception unwinding and a tail call
that reuses its activation must close the affected cells at the
same boundary. Captured bindings read after suspension require
that the coroutine's saved stack remain reachable, or that the
slots be moved through a protocol preserving open cell access.

\section{Make Stack Scans and Recovery Agree With the Layout}

A tagged value stack can expose all initialized live slots
to a precise collector; an unboxed stack may require a
per-instruction map. Descriptors holding closure or code
references and a pending return value also need tracing as
appropriate. For the compiler/native boundary and stack
maps, see \Cref{ch:ext-411}. An open upvalue can cause a
suspended stack to be kept alive, and a closed cell can point
to a heap object. Defining the tracing policy for each variant
prevents either from becoming an invisible source of live
references. If a write barrier or allocation may collect,
keep the return value and close operation's inputs rooted
until publication. The pop-and-root step must not expose a
safepoint between removing the result from its old root slot
and registering its temporary root.

Exercise the factory with frame growth between capture and
return, call both closures, then create a second factory
activation with the same slot numbers. The first pair must
agree on its updated value without affecting the second
pair. Add nested captures forwarded through a closure that
never reads the variable itself. Finally force an early
unwind through a frame after its closure has escaped; test
that the escaped closure still uses the right value.

\section{Choose Capture Storage From Workloads}

Eagerly allocating heap boxes for the compiler's marked
captured locals simplifies frame teardown, but pays even
on paths that never actually create a closure. An open-cell
scheme pays when capture occurs and on closing, with an index
to preserve aliasing. A sorted linked list can make closing
a suffix cheap in a VM with very few live open cells; finding
an arbitrary capture can take a linear scan. An ordered tree
makes both locating a capture and updating the index
logarithmic but produces more allocation and pointer traffic.
Compare actual numbers of simultaneously open captures,
closures sharing cells, and cells closed per unwind before
choosing. Test equality of effects, not only closure count:
one extra box allocated per closure can be a semantic bug
as well as a memory regression.

\section{Check the Stack Shape at Instruction Boundaries}

Suppose a caller has a saved temporary in slot 4, a callee in
slot 5, and an integer argument in slot 6. One calling
convention sets restore depth to 5 and reuses slot 6 as the
callee's first local, leaving slot 5 occupied by the call's
closure until return. If a capture refers to slot 6 and the
callee updates it from 40 to 41, a return closes that cell
with 41, truncates to depth 5, and writes the result at slot
5. The caller's temporary in slot 4 is untouched. Moving
frame metadata outside the value vector does not eliminate
the need to track the two separate boundaries: local base 6
and restore depth 5.

An interpreter can check a bytecode function before execution
by walking its control-flow graph with the operand depth at
each instruction entry. For a binary integer operation,
require at least two operands above the operand base and
propagate one fewer to the successor. A conditional jump
that pops its predicate takes the same reduced depth to both
targets. At a join, reject conflicting incoming depths unless
the instruction set explicitly permits them. Check each local
and capture operand against its own descriptor, not against the
current global stack length. A deep caller stack must not
make an invalid operand in a shallow callee acceptable. Add
an independent check that return paths root the pending
result before any operation which can collect.
